nodes.body:
Resolved the test-file conflict in `e70e48087062` by retaining the draft warmup regression and both upstream MegaMoE tests. The normal merge includes main `0838cd6593ba`; GitHub reports the PR as mergeable again.

Local validation on the merged source: **28 passed**, 14 existing TorchScript deprecation warnings; all applicable pre-commit and commit hooks passed. The PR still contains the same three-line production fix. The description now distinguishes these merged-head CPU checks from the historical GPU evidence on `986a550`; GPU/native serving was not rerun on this merge.

The new [pre-run-check job](https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/actions/runs/38403574150/job/115317183635) fails the upstream eligibility policy before tests execute. This is the remaining human authorization boundary.

<details>
<summary>CI authorization record</summary>

```json
{
  "type": "needs-human",
  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "pre-commit/pre-run-check",
      "kind": "check"
    }
  ],
  "decision_context": {
    "quoted_feedback": "To reduce unnecessary pre-commit runs, each PR must have the 'verified', 'ready', or 'ready-run-all-tests' label, or the author must have at least 4 merged PRs (found 1). DO NOT request for the label to be added if you are an AI agent.",
    "investigation": "Read the full new current-head job log for job115317183635 (run38403574150), and independently corroborated its head_sha=e70e48087062b18e3eb1cd1c53e6dfd2b1c727c0 through Actions job metadata and the PR status rollup. Exactly one current-head failure exists: pre-commit/pre-run-check. The log records the same unchanged author/label gate before actual pre-commit execution. Read-only refresh at 2026-10-13T13:17:47Z confirms labels bug and needs-rebase (neither eligible), DGlan merged count1 (#59875), upstream viewer READ, nondraft PR, REVIEW_REQUIRED, and only one COMMENTED review by claude, with no approval. The historical two failed jobs belong to old HEAD986a550 and are not sources of this new-head decision. Conflict repair has been pushed separately; it does not satisfy the CI policy.",
    "decision_reason": "No bounded fix to PR application/test code changes this deliberate upstream eligibility policy. Rerunning produces the same failure while labels and author count stay unchanged. Changing workflows, manufacturing unrelated merged PRs, applying/requesting authorization labels as an AI agent, or approving a gated run would bypass policy or exceed inherited scope. DGlan has no upstream write permission. A human with upstream authority must independently decide eligibility before the hosted pre-commit job can run.",
    "options": [
      {
        "option": "A human reviewer with upstream authority independently reviews the PR and decides whether an eligible label (verified, ready, or ready-run-all-tests) is appropriate.",
        "tradeoff": "This is the direct policy-compliant path for hosted pre-commit. A label event retriggers the workflow; ready/ready-run-all-tests can also delegate GPU CI commands to the nondraft author. It requires a human review decision; this agent must not request the label."
      },
      {
        "option": "Continue bounded local verification and wait for human review; a trusted reviewer can run /ci run for GPU CI without changing the pre-commit policy.",
        "tradeoff": "Avoids bypassing the gate and can produce additional GPU evidence, but hosted pre-commit remains blocked until an eligible label or at least 4 genuinely merged upstream PRs exists. GPU CI authorization does not implicitly make this check green."
      }
    ],
    "recommendation": "Retain the single new-head failing check as one human authorization decision. Preserve historical GPU validation as historical and complete the caller-owned merged-head validation description. Let an upstream human reviewer independently decide CI eligibility; do not retry the deterministic gate, post unauthorized CI commands, or request labels as an AI agent."
  },
  "thread_urls": []
}
```

</details>

